          "text": "The implementation looks correct. Let me verify against the requirements:\n\n1. Return a list keeping the first occurrence of each value, in input order, using equality — yes: `for item in values: if item not in result: result.append(item)`. Membership test `in` on a list uses `==` (with an `is` fast path), so order and equality semantics are preserved.\n2. Accept any iterable, including generators — yes, we just iterate `for item in values`.\n3. Support unhashable values such as lists and dicts — yes, list membership doesn't require hashing.\n4. Do not mutate the input values — correct; we only read and build a new list.\n5. Preserve the public API — the function name and signature are unchanged; `reversed_copy` untouched.\n\nTrade-off note: O(n²) worst case due to linear membership scanning; that's the price for supporting unhashable values with strict equality semantics. A hybrid hashable fast-path would complicate the code and could introduce subtle equality edge cases (e.g., unhashable values that compare equal to hashable seen values via custom `__eq__`); the simple version is unambiguously correct.\n\nI'm done. Provide the finish action with a brief explanation and source reference.",
